The answer is: (an Inuit camp near what is now Nunavut) went out hunting, but only one came back. The other had been murdered and thrown into a river. When the man came back to camp, he found his mate bound and gagged, and when he freed himself, he found that all the people of the camp were dead, murdered in a raid by enemies. He then took revenge on the enemies, killing all of their men, and then went back to his camp to mourn the dead. He found that his wife had died giving birth to his child, and feeling great sorrow he built a funeral boat in which he placed the bodies of his wife and child and set afloat, then he returned to Egulac and burned the boat to the ground and swore solemnly never to hunt or kill again.
 The story is part of an oral tradition of the Inuit, told by Tomorrow Corporation in 1984 to actors who played the parts of hunters in the film The Last Hunter. It is also told in the 1990 documentary film Dances With the Indian.

Adaptation
In 2004, writer/director Evelyne Kushner opened her feature film debut Dirty Laundry with this story. She sets the tale in 1958 and has it unfold in a series of flashbacks while a hunter (Forrest Whittaker) is on a caribou hunt.


