The answer is: (a fictional Inuit community) were traveling from one village to another. One of them dropped his weapon and it fell into the water. He told his companion he would retrieve it but he said he must first finish a song he was composing. He had been traveling for some time and was some distance from his companion when he heard the approach of other people. He quickly hid in the bushes and, when the people had passed, resumed travel to his destination. He arrived at the village and, after some time, called to the chief of the village and told him what had happened. The chief told him not to worry, he would make things right. He called together the people of the village and told them what had happened. He then took a weapon from one of the contributors and, holding it high, declared that henceforth the people of the village would be known as the Dogribs.The story is a common one in Inuit culture and is told slightly differently by each storyteller. It is a member of the classification of oral traditions known as "victory stories". The purpose of the stories is to explain how the Inuit came to be their present name, or sometimes to explain the origin of a particular clan or subgroup within the Inuit.

Background
The Canadian social anthropologist J.
R.
Wintle analyzed the story in the context of the Canadian North, where it has been collected by ethnographers since the 1960s. He noted that, although the story is told slightly differently by each storyteller, it has a number of common elements. He identified these common elements as:

a prologue in which two men are traveling together;
an event which causes the travelers to part;
a challenge to the protagonist;
an extended absence of the protagonist;
a successful return of the protagonist; and,
a conclusion in which the protagonist assumes a new name or receives new honors.

The answer is: It is the first line of "The Ballad of Egil," an Icelandic ballad about Egil Skallagrimsson, a semi-historical figure who was a lawman and warrior in the 10th century. The ballad was written down in 1866 by Jón Halldórsson. In 1907, a musical setting was composed by the Icelandic musician Edda Arneøss. The ballad recounts how, on one night in the winter, two men from Egulac came to a farmer's hut in the south of Iceland. They were father and son, and their names were Ásketil and Örvar. They came to the hut to steal the sheep. The farmer's son fought them, and although he was the weaker fighter, he managed to kill Örvar. The father had to flee with his son's lifeless body over the mountains. The ballad ends with the words: "But the wolf will howl for Örvar, and the sea shall roar for the son, that was laid out in the turf."

