The answer is: (a fictional Inuit community) were traveling from a neighboring community when they came across a group of four women who were stuck in the mud, unable to move their vehicle. The two men were described as “rough men, rough men of the North”, and in the tradition of Arctic explorers, they held the four women for four days until rescue could come. That rescue never came, and the two men eventually raped and killed the women, leaving their bodies in the mud for the inhabitants of Egulac to discover. This story is told by a woman named Allasha in Peter Haining's book Arctic Kiss: A Novel of Intrigue and Love in the Arctic Circle.

The case of the women's deaths was investigated by Canadian police and, in a rare instance of prosecution for rape in an Arctic community, the two men were convicted. However, they were sentenced to only four years in prison, despite the fact that under Canadian law, rape was punishable by up to 10 years in prison at the time. The women's families were outraged, and activists spoke out against what they saw as a very light sentence.

The story of the deaths has been recounted in a number of works. It was recounted in Peter Stark's 2007 book Dog Team, a work of creative nonfiction, and in Robert McGinty's 2008 book End of the World: Ghosts of Muskox Island, a work of fiction.

