The answer is: (a fictional Inuit community) were fighting with spears in the night;
The sky was clear, the moon was bright;
They fought until they saw the light;
There in the distance shone a light.
It was the Northern Lights,
Dancing in the cold, clear night.
One man turned to the other and said, "This is why we fight;
This is what we fight for."And they kept right on fighting; they never made peace;
They fought through the ages, until the end of time;
And when the end came, there was only ice;
And where they had fought, there was nothing but the Northern Lights.

Background
This poem was composed by a Canadian poet, Susanne L. Pritchard, and sets a scene of an Inuit battle that continues forever, fighting for an unstated goal. The battle is eternal and takes place in the cold, with the Northern Lights being the only source of color in the scene. The poem was first published in 1978 and has been reprinted multiple times. It has been set to music by various artists, most notably by the Canadian musician Wade MacNeil.

Performance
The most famous recording of the poem is by the Canadian musician Wade MacNeil, as part of his 2009 album For the Love of Humanity. On this recording, the poem is recited by Canadian actor Tom McCamus over hauntingly beautiful music. The recording begins with the sounds of an Inuit drum, which accompanies the recitation of the poem. After the poem is recited, the drum fades out, leaving only the sounds of the Northern Lights, which are created by layering many guitar tracks. The entire piece is around five minutes in length.


